            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-27613",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2025-27613"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2025-27613?s=debian&n=git&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=11&vr=%3C1%3A2.30.2-1%2Bdeb11u5",
              "help": {
                "text": "Gitk is a Tcl/Tk based Git history browser. Starting with 1.7.0, when a user clones an untrusted repository and runs gitk without additional command arguments, files for which the user has write permission can be created and truncated. The option Support per-file encoding must have been enabled before in Gitk's Preferences. This option is disabled by default. The same happens when Show origin of this line is used in the main window (regardless of whether Support per-file encoding is enabled or not). This vulnerability is fixed in 2.43.7, 2.44.4, 2.45.4, 2.46.4, 2.47.3, 2.48.2, 2.49.1, and 2.50.1.\n\n---\n- git 1:2.50.1-0.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1108983)\n[trixie] - git 1:2.47.3-0+deb13u1\n[bookworm] - git 1:2.39.5-0+deb12u3\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/git/xmqq5xg2wrd1.fsf@gitster.g/\nMerge commit: https://github.com/git/git/commit/d61cfed2c23705fbeb9c0d08f59e75ee08738950 (v2.43.7)\n",
                "markdown": "> Gitk is a Tcl/Tk based Git history browser. Starting with 1.7.0, when a user clones an untrusted repository and runs gitk without additional command arguments, files for which the user has write permission can be created and truncated. The option Support per-file encoding must have been enabled before in Gitk's Preferences. This option is disabled by default. The same happens when Show origin of this line is used in the main window (regardless of whether Support per-file encoding is enabled or not). This vulnerability is fixed in 2.43.7, 2.44.4, 2.45.4, 2.46.4, 2.47.3, 2.48.2, 2.49.1, and 2.50.1.\n\n---\n- git 1:2.50.1-0.1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1108983)\n[trixie] - git 1:2.47.3-0+deb13u1\n[bookworm] - git 1:2.39.5-0+deb12u3\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/git/xmqq5xg2wrd1.fsf@gitster.g/\nMerge commit: https://github.com/git/git/commit/d61cfed2c23705fbeb9c0d08f59e75ee08738950 (v2.43.7)\n\n|                                                                                                           |\n|Package         pkg:deb/debian/git@1%3A2.30.2-1%2Bdeb11u2?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11  |\n|Affected range  <1:2.30.2-1+deb11u5                                                                        |\n|Fixed version   1:2.30.2-1+deb11u5                                                                         |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1:2.30.2-1+deb11u5",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "1:2.30.2-1+deb11u5",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/git@1%3A2.30.2-1%2Bdeb11u2?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.6",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-28322",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-28322"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2023-28322?s=debian&n=curl&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=11&vr=%3C7.74.0-1.3%2Bdeb11u9",
              "help": {
                "text": "An information disclosure vulnerability exists in curl <v8.1.0 when doing HTTP(S) transfers, libcurl might erroneously use the read callback (`CURLOPT_READFUNCTION`) to ask for data to send, even when the `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS` option has been set, if the same handle previously wasused to issue a `PUT` request which used that callback. This flaw may surprise the application and cause it to misbehave and either send off the wrong data or use memory after free or similar in the second transfer. The problem exists in the logic for a reused handle when it is (expected to be) changed from a PUT to a POST.\n\n---\n- curl 7.88.1-10 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1036239)\n[bullseye] - curl 7.74.0-1.3+deb11u9\nhttps://curl.se/docs/CVE-2023-28322.html\nIntroduced by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/546572da0457f37c698c02d0a08d90fdfcbeedec (curl-7_7)\nFixed by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/7815647d6582c0a4900be2e1de6c5e61272c496b (curl-8_1_0)\n",
                "markdown": "> An information disclosure vulnerability exists in curl <v8.1.0 when doing HTTP(S) transfers, libcurl might erroneously use the read callback (`CURLOPT_READFUNCTION`) to ask for data to send, even when the `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS` option has been set, if the same handle previously wasused to issue a `PUT` request which used that callback. This flaw may surprise the application and cause it to misbehave and either send off the wrong data or use memory after free or similar in the second transfer. The problem exists in the logic for a reused handle when it is (expected to be) changed from a PUT to a POST.\n\n---\n- curl 7.88.1-10 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1036239)\n[bullseye] - curl 7.74.0-1.3+deb11u9\nhttps://curl.se/docs/CVE-2023-28322.html\nIntroduced by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/546572da0457f37c698c02d0a08d90fdfcbeedec (curl-7_7)\nFixed by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/7815647d6582c0a4900be2e1de6c5e61272c496b (curl-8_1_0)\n\n|                                                                                                          |\n|Package         pkg:deb/debian/curl@7.74.0-1.3%2Bdeb11u7?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11  |\n|Affected range  <7.74.0-1.3+deb11u9                                                                       |\n|Fixed version   7.74.0-1.3+deb11u9                                                                        |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<7.74.0-1.3+deb11u9",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "7.74.0-1.3+deb11u9",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/curl@7.74.0-1.3%2Bdeb11u7?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-38546",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-38546"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2023-38546?s=debian&n=curl&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=11&vr=%3C7.74.0-1.3%2Bdeb11u10",
              "help": {
                "text": "This flaw allows an attacker to insert cookies at will into a running program using libcurl, if the specific series of conditions are met.  libcurl performs transfers. In its API, an application creates \"easy handles\" that are the individual handles for single transfers.  libcurl provides a function call that duplicates en easy handle called [curl_easy_duphandle](https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl_easy_duphandle.html).  If a transfer has cookies enabled when the handle is duplicated, the cookie-enable state is also cloned - but without cloning the actual cookies. If the source handle did not read any cookies from a specific file on disk, the cloned version of the handle would instead store the file name as `none` (using the four ASCII letters, no quotes).  Subsequent use of the cloned handle that does not explicitly set a source to load cookies from would then inadvertently load cookies from a file named `none` - if such a file exists and is readable in the current directory of the program using libcurl. And if using the correct file format of course.\n\n---\n- curl 8.3.0-3\nhttps://curl.se/docs/CVE-2023-38546.html\nIntroduced by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/74d5a6fb3b9a96d9fa51ba90996e94c878ebd151 (curl-7_9_1)\nFixed by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/61275672b46d9abb3285740467b882e22ed75da8 (curl-8_4_0)\n",
                "markdown": "> This flaw allows an attacker to insert cookies at will into a running program using libcurl, if the specific series of conditions are met.  libcurl performs transfers. In its API, an application creates \"easy handles\" that are the individual handles for single transfers.  libcurl provides a function call that duplicates en easy handle called [curl_easy_duphandle](https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl_easy_duphandle.html).  If a transfer has cookies enabled when the handle is duplicated, the cookie-enable state is also cloned - but without cloning the actual cookies. If the source handle did not read any cookies from a specific file on disk, the cloned version of the handle would instead store the file name as `none` (using the four ASCII letters, no quotes).  Subsequent use of the cloned handle that does not explicitly set a source to load cookies from would then inadvertently load cookies from a file named `none` - if such a file exists and is readable in the current directory of the program using libcurl. And if using the correct file format of course.\n\n---\n- curl 8.3.0-3\nhttps://curl.se/docs/CVE-2023-38546.html\nIntroduced by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/74d5a6fb3b9a96d9fa51ba90996e94c878ebd151 (curl-7_9_1)\nFixed by: https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/61275672b46d9abb3285740467b882e22ed75da8 (curl-8_4_0)\n\n|                                                                                                          |\n|Package         pkg:deb/debian/curl@7.74.0-1.3%2Bdeb11u7?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11  |\n|Affected range  <7.74.0-1.3+deb11u10                                                                      |\n|Fixed version   7.74.0-1.3+deb11u10                                                                       |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<7.74.0-1.3+deb11u10",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "7.74.0-1.3+deb11u10",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:deb/debian/curl@7.74.0-1.3%2Bdeb11u7?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-22873",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2025-22873"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2025-22873?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3C1.23.9",
              "help": {
                "text": "It was possible to improperly access the parent directory of an os.Root by opening a filename ending in \"../\". For example, Root.Open(\"../\") would open the parent directory of the Root. This escape only permits opening the parent directory itself, not ancestors of the parent or files contained within the parent.\n",
                "markdown": "> It was possible to improperly access the parent directory of an os.Root by opening a filename ending in \"../\". For example, Root.Open(\"../\") would open the parent directory of the Root. This escape only permits opening the parent directory itself, not ancestors of the parent or files contained within the parent.\n\n|                                          |\n|Package         pkg:golang/stdlib@1.19.8  |\n|Affected range  <1.23.9                   |\n|Fixed version   1.23.9                    |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.23.9",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "1.23.9",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.19.8"
                ],
                "security-severity": "3.8",
                "tags": [
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2024-32021",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2024-32021"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2024-32021?s=debian&n=git&ns=debian&t=deb&osn=debian&osv=11&vr=%3C1%3A2.30.2-1%2Bdeb11u3",
              "help": {
                "text": "Git is a revision control system. Prior to versions 2.45.1, 2.44.1, 2.43.4, 2.42.2, 2.41.1, 2.40.2, and 2.39.4, when cloning a local source repository that contains symlinks via the filesystem, Git may create hardlinks to arbitrary user-readable files on the same filesystem as the target repository in the `objects/` directory. Cloning a local repository over the filesystem may creating hardlinks to arbitrary user-owned files on the same filesystem in the target Git repository's `objects/` directory. When cloning a repository over the filesystem (without explicitly specifying the `file://` protocol or `--no-local`), the optimizations for local cloning will be used, which include attempting to hard link the object files instead of copying them. While the code includes checks against symbolic links in the source repository, which were added during the fix for CVE-2022-39253, these checks can still be raced because the hard link operation ultimately follows symlinks. If the object on the filesystem appears as a file during the check, and then a symlink during the operation, this will allow the adversary to bypass the check and create hardlinks in the destination objects directory to arbitrary, user-readable files. The problem has been patched in versions 2.45.1, 2.44.1, 2.43.4, 2.42.2, 2.41.1, 2.40.2, and 2.39.4.\n\n---\n- git 1:2.45.1-1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1071160)\nhttps://github.com/git/git/security/advisories/GHSA-mvxm-9j2h-qjx7\n",
                "markdown": "> Git is a revision control system. Prior to versions 2.45.1, 2.44.1, 2.43.4, 2.42.2, 2.41.1, 2.40.2, and 2.39.4, when cloning a local source repository that contains symlinks via the filesystem, Git may create hardlinks to arbitrary user-readable files on the same filesystem as the target repository in the `objects/` directory. Cloning a local repository over the filesystem may creating hardlinks to arbitrary user-owned files on the same filesystem in the target Git repository's `objects/` directory. When cloning a repository over the filesystem (without explicitly specifying the `file://` protocol or `--no-local`), the optimizations for local cloning will be used, which include attempting to hard link the object files instead of copying them. While the code includes checks against symbolic links in the source repository, which were added during the fix for CVE-2022-39253, these checks can still be raced because the hard link operation ultimately follows symlinks. If the object on the filesystem appears as a file during the check, and then a symlink during the operation, this will allow the adversary to bypass the check and create hardlinks in the destination objects directory to arbitrary, user-readable files. The problem has been patched in versions 2.45.1, 2.44.1, 2.43.4, 2.42.2, 2.41.1, 2.40.2, and 2.39.4.\n\n---\n- git 1:2.45.1-1 (bug https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=1071160)\nhttps://github.com/git/git/security/advisories/GHSA-mvxm-9j2h-qjx7\n\n|                                                                                                           |\n|Package         pkg:deb/debian/git@1%3A2.30.2-1%2Bdeb11u2?os_distro=bullseye&os_name=debian&os_version=11  |\n|Affected range  <1:2.30.2-1+deb11u3                                                                        |\n|Fixed version   1:2.30.2-1+deb11u3                                                                         |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1:2.30.2-1+deb11u3",
                "cvssV3_severity": "LOW",
                "fixed_version": "1:2.30.2-1+deb11u3",
